- Root-cause chain: `repair.py` appended a synthetic tool result after a new user message had already been persisted, and `display_metadata.kernel_run_id` was read as `metadata.kernel_run_id`, so existing `tool_start` could be misclassified as `not_started`. The restart origin itself remained unproven; preserve it as unknown rather than blaming a model, user, or external program. [Task 1]
- The isolated repair checkpoint is `D:\Atlas\.worktrees\fix-model-capabilities-20261018` on `fix/model-capabilities-20261018`, commit `a799a8a863d447cf2c8d7d8faa69b3af1114f859`. Before continuing, read `Atlas修复进度与续接-2026-10-18.md`, then inspect worktree status because post-checkpoint changes include `tool_node.py`, two tests, and `work/`. [Task 2]
- Two open repair gaps are material: `_openai_payload` filters compatible `reasoning_content`/`reasoning_details` fields, and old detached writers without a handoff note can keep modifying files while `_pending_background_writers()` permits a verification credential; `end_run()` only cancels non-detached processes. Repair the first per target-provider contract, not by indiscriminately forwarding non-standard fields. [Task 2]
- In `D:\Atlas`, runtime tray health polling is `/api/health` with a 1500ms timeout every 5 seconds; two failures call `startBackend()`. The 700ms value belongs to startup/reuse probing. `backendProcess.ts` can ultimately run `taskkill /PID ... /T /F` after 30 seconds and source review found no active-run guard. [Task 3]
- Keep service health, process liveness, and task state distinct. Public Codex App Server shows `/readyz`, `/healthz`, thread/turn/item lifecycle, per-turn `turn/interrupt`, and overload retry; OpenCode separates `/global/health`, SSE events, async prompt, and session abort. These prove public API patterns, not the complete Codex Desktop watchdog implementation. [Task 3]

## Failures and how to do differently

- Symptom: resumed provider call returns `No tool output found for tool call` -> cause: illegal tool-call/tool-result ordering during repair and mismatched metadata key -> fix the recovery sequence/identity check, then prove it with the minimal three-message protocol plus the relevant Atlas recovery path. [Task 1]
- Symptom: a health report appears to prove a self-kill root cause -> cause: it combines correlated signs (health failure, restart, PID kill, active run) without the actual linkage -> independently join the health event, restart request, killed PID, and affected run before calling the causal chain confirmed. Do not conflate output truncation, final-message persistence, and duplicate persistence. [Task 3]
- Symptom: a broad repair looks green -> cause: focused checks and a checkpoint were mistaken for all live/provider/OS lifecycle acceptance -> keep two protocol/detached-writer gaps, real-model multi-turn behavior, and OS updater/shutdown handshake explicitly open until directly tested. [Task 2]
- Symptom: a reliability request expands into a sweeping plan with no immediate outcome -> pivot to one concrete fault such as “health timeout cannot kill an active run,” protect the dirty host through a worktree/baseline, agree a short acceptance condition, and implement only that closed loop. [Task 3]


